request_text:
The new implementation can create inheritable handles on Windows. Since `Process.Start` creates a process that inherits all inheritable handles, we need to prevent from accidental handle inheritance.

We should use the reader-writer lock. The following `lock` object:

https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/31c3ed919e1a04ce24ed75ca76d69b7a8653b344/src/libraries/System.Diagnostics.Process/src/System/Diagnostics/Process.Windows.cs#L19

Needs to be turned into `ReaderWriterLockSlim`, just like on Unix:

https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/31c3ed919e1a04ce24ed75ca76d69b7a8653b344/src/libraries/System.Diagnostics.Process/src/System/Diagnostics/Process.Unix.cs#L21

(so actually both can be moved to `Process.cs` and made internal).

Then, the existing usage of the `object` lock needs to be turned into writer lock:

https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/31c3ed919e1a04ce24ed75ca76d69b7a8653b344/src/libraries/System.Diagnostics.Process/src/System/Diagnostics/Process.Windows.cs#L456

And here in the new code (I mean SafeProcessHandle.Windows.cs), we need to acquire reader lock. Please don't add any level of nesting. Just introduce a new method and do sth like this:

```csharp
private static unsafe SafeProcessHandle StartCore(ProcessStartOptions options, SafeFileHandle inputHandle, SafeFileHandle outputHandle, SafeFileHandle errorHandle, bool createSuspended)
{
    Process.s_processStartLock.EnterReadLock();
    try
    {
        return StartCoreSerialized(options, inputHandle, outputHandle, errorHandle, createSuspended);
    }
    }
    finally
    {
        Process.s_processStartLock.ExitReadLock();
    }
}

private static unsafe SafeProcessHandle StartCoreSerialized(ProcessStartOptions options, SafeFileHandle inputHandle, SafeFileHandle outputHandle, SafeFileHandle errorHandle, bool createSuspended)
```

