<issue_title>Force-unwrap on UUID and try! on JSON in Database crash the extension on any DB corruption</issue_title>
<issue_description>## Bug

Four methods in `Database.swift` will crash `opfilter` (the privileged system extension) if the SQLite file contains any malformed data:

```swift
// Crashes if id column is not a valid UUID string
private func ruleFromRow(_ stmt: OpaquePointer) -> FAARule {
    FAARule(
        id: UUID(uuidString: columnText(stmt, 0))!,  // ← force unwrap
        ...
    )
}

// Same in allowlistEntryFromRow
id: UUID(uuidString: columnText(stmt, 0))!,  // ← force unwrap

// Crash if stored JSON is malformed
private func decodeStringArray(_ json: String) -> [String] {
    try! JSONDecoder().decode([String].self, from: json.data(using: .utf8)!)
}
private func decodeSignatureArray(_ json: String) -> [ProcessSignature] {
    try! JSONDecoder().decode([ProcessSignature].self, from: json.data(using: .utf8)!)
}
```

These crash `opfilter` — not the GUI app. The extension would be killed and file access enforcement would stop entirely. Triggers include:

- Partial write corruption (power loss mid-write before WAL is fully checkpointed)
- Any future schema migration that writes a malformed intermediate value
- Manual inspection/editing of the database

## Fix

`ruleFromRow` and `allowlistEntryFromRow` should use `guard let id = UUID(...)` and skip the row (logging a fault) rather than crashing. The decode helpers should use `try?` with a fallback of `[]` and log a fault. A corrupt single row should not take down the entire extension.

## Severity
**High** — any DB corruption disables file access enforcement silently (extension crashes, no more ES filtering).</issue_description>

