request_text:
Create a PR that Summary
Three locations build a filesystem path with filepath.Join(base, userInfluenced) and then read from it without verifying the result stays under base. None is exploitable in the current call graph — inputs originate from git operations on developer-specified repositories — but all would become exploitable if the helper were reused with less-trusted input.

This is a hardening request, not an active vulnerability.

Severity
Defense in Depth — no exploit path exists at the reviewed commit; this is proactive hardening against future refactors that might introduce less-trusted input.

(Reclassified from Low on 2026-03-25 after review against the ProdSec severity guide. Matches DiD definition: "areas where we could improve resilience to security vulnerabilities… expecting that one may fail." No fix deadline, but worth tracking.)

Affected Code
What	Why it's currently safe	Permalink
download_workflow.go: read file after sparse-checkout	path comes from the developer's import spec; clone is into a fresh MkdirTemp dir	https://github.com/github/gh-aw/blob/5fe53c0a8502459257d97092c5031142fa44968a/pkg/cli/download_workflow.go#L140
remote_fetch.go: read file after git clone	Same as above	https://github.com/github/gh-aw/blob/5fe53c0a8502459257d97092c5031142fa44968a/pkg/parser/remote_fetch.go#L547
tar.go: exact-match on tar entry name	Caller controls path, not the archive; single-file extraction, nothing written to disk	https://github.com/github/gh-aw/blob/5fe53c0a8502459257d97092c5031142fa44968a/pkg/fileutil/tar.go#L32
Root Cause
// download_workflow.go:140
filePath := filepath.Join(tmpDir, path)
content, err := os.ReadFile(filePath)   // no boundary check

// remote_fetch.go:547 — identical pattern
filePath := filepath.Join(tmpDir, path)
content, err := os.ReadFile(filePath)

// tar.go:32 — exact string match, no path normalisation
if header.Name == path {
    return io.ReadAll(tr)
}
filepath.Join("/tmp/xyz", "../../../etc/passwd") → /etc/passwd. The join normalises but does not contain.

Steps to Reproduce
This is not directly exploitable today. To demonstrate the pattern is unsafe:

// Hypothetical: if path were ever attacker-controlled
tmpDir := "/tmp/gh-aw-clone-abc"
path := "../../../etc/hostname"
filePath := filepath.Join(tmpDir, path)
// filePath == "/etc/hostname"
os.ReadFile(filePath)  // reads outside tmpDir
Contrast with the correctly-hardened zip extractor in the same codebase:
https://github.com/github/gh-aw/blob/5fe53c0a8502459257d97092c5031142fa44968a/pkg/cli/logs_download.go#L364-L380

Impact
None today. If any of these code paths is later refactored to accept a path from a less-trusted source (a downloaded manifest, an API response, a remote tar entry), it becomes an arbitrary-file-read.

Recommended Fix
Add a small helper and apply it at all three sites:

// pkg/fileutil/path.go
func MustBeWithin(base, candidate string) error {
    absBase, err := filepath.Abs(base)
    if err != nil {
        return err
    }
    absCand, err := filepath.Abs(candidate)
    if err != nil {
        return err
    }
    rel, err := filepath.Rel(absBase, absCand)
    if err != nil || rel == ".." || strings.HasPrefix(rel, ".."+string(filepath.Separator)) {
        return fmt.Errorf("path %q escapes base directory %q", candidate, base)
    }
    return nil
}
Usage:

filePath := filepath.Join(tmpDir, path)
if err := fileutil.MustBeWithin(tmpDir, filePath); err != nil {
    return nil, err
}
content, err := os.ReadFile(filePath)
For tar.go, additionally reject path containing .. or a leading / on entry.

Related
Standalone hardening request. The zip extractor at logs_download.go:364-380 is a good reference implementation already in the codebase.

