&gt;   validateBackendUrl(trimmed);   // throws if invalid
&gt;   _backendUrl = trimmed;         // only reached if validation passes
&gt; };
&gt; ```
&gt; 
&gt; Mutation ordering is **correct**: `_backendUrl` is only assigned after `validateBackendUrl(trimmed)` returns normally. If validation throws, `_backendUrl` retains its previous value. This is the correct fail-safe pattern.
&gt; 
&gt; `validateBackendUrl()` correctly:
&gt; 
&gt; * Rejects malformed URLs via `new URL()` exception
&gt; * Rejects `javascript:`, `file:`, `data:`, `blob:`, `ftp:`, `ws:`, `wss:`, and any non-`http:`/`https:` scheme
&gt; * Accepts any `http://` or `https://` URL intentionally (remote server support)
&gt; * Does not restrict to loopback (correct for this use case)
&gt; 
&gt; `normalizeServerUrl()` adds protocol before calling `setBackendUrl()`; this does not bypass validation since the normalized URL is still passed through `validateBackendUrl()` inside `setBackendUrl()`. All downstream fetch calls (`fetchGraph`, `fetchRepos`, `streamSSE`, etc.) use `_backendUrl` as a base prefix — no additional validation is needed since the module-level state is always validated before storage.
&gt; 
&gt; ### Log-injection assessment
&gt; **`bridge-db.ts`:** The diff shows the old call was:
&gt; 
&gt; ```ts
&gt; bridgeLogger.debug({ groupDir, err: lastErr, attempts: ... }, '...');
